'''Vlade Divac''' ({{lang-sr-Cyrl|Владе Дивац}}, {{IPA|sh|ʋlǎːde dǐːʋats|pron}}; born February 3, 1968) is a Serbian professional [[basketball]] executive and a former player who was most recently the vice president of basketball operations and general manager of the [[Sacramento Kings]] of the [[National Basketball Association]] (NBA).

At {{height|ft=7|in=1}},<ref>{{cite web |title=Vlade Divac Stats |url=https://www.basketball-reference.com/players/d/divacvl01.html |website=Basketball-Reference.com |access-date=29 January 2020 |language=en |archive-date=April 4, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130404060243/http://www.basketball-reference.com/players/d/divacvl01.html |url-status=dead }}</ref> Divac played [[center (basketball)|center]] and, unlike many centers, was known for his passing skills. He was among the first group of European basketball players to transfer to the NBA in the late 1980s and was named one of the [[50 Greatest EuroLeague Contributors]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.euroleague.net/item/24016 |title=Experts decide European Club Basketball's 50 greatest contributors – 50 YEARS – Welcome to EUROLEAGUE BASKETBALL |publisher=Euroleague.net |access-date=January 24, 2013}}</ref> He is one of seven players in NBA history to record 13,000 [[Point (basketball)|points]], 9,000 [[Rebound (basketball)|rebounds]], 3,000 [[Assist (basketball)|assists]], and 1,500 blocked shots, along with [[Kareem Abdul-Jabbar]], [[Hakeem Olajuwon]], [[Shaquille O'Neal]], [[Tim Duncan]], [[Kevin Garnett]], and [[Pau Gasol]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.nba.com/lakers/news/divac_retires_051018.html |title=Vlade Divac Announces Retirement; Accepts Position With Lakers |publisher=National Basketball Association |access-date=January 24, 2013}}</ref><ref group=n>The NBA did not record blocked shots before the 1973–74 season, so earlier players such as [[Bill Russell]] and [[Wilt Chamberlain]] probably had similar career achievements.</ref> Divac was also the first player born and trained outside the United States to play in over 1,000 games in the NBA. Divac was named to the [[FIBA All-Time EuroStars Team]] in 2007. On August 20, 2010, he was inducted into the [[FIBA Hall of Fame]] in recognition of his play in international competition.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.halloffame.fiba.com/pages/eng/hof/news/p/newsid/42798/arti.html |title=FIBA announces 2010 Hall of Fame Class |publisher=[[FIBA]] |date=August 20, 2010 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101019073536/http://www.halloffame.fiba.com/pages/eng/hof/news/p/newsid/42798/arti.html |archive-date=October 19, 2010 }}</ref> He was elected to the [[Naismith Memorial Basketball Hall of Fame]] in 2019.<ref name="hof">{{Cite web |url=https://www.nba.com/article/2019/04/06/2019-naismith-basketball-hall-fame-announcement |title=Divac, Sikma, Moncrief headline Hall of Fame Class of 2019 |publisher=[[National Basketball Association]] |date=April 6, 2019}}</ref>

Divac is a [[humanitarian]], helping children in his native country of Serbia and in Africa.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.nba.com/news/divac_youcantoo_070922.html |title=Divac Creates New Team With "You Can Too" Campaign |publisher=National Basketball Association |date=September 22, 2007 |access-date=January 24, 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071011022055/http://www.nba.com/news/divac_youcantoo_070922.html |archive-date=October 11, 2007 |url-status=dead }}</ref> In October 2008, he was appointed as government adviser in Serbia for humanitarian issues.<ref>{{Cite web |date=October 16, 2008 |title=Ex-NBA star appointed government adviser |url=https://sports.yahoo.com/nba/news?slug=ap-divac-serbianadviser&prov=ap&type=lgns |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081020220122/http://sports.yahoo.com/nba/news?slug=ap-divac-serbianadviser&prov=ap&type=lgns |archive-date=October 20, 2008 |access-date=October 17, 2008 |website=Yahoo! Sports |agency=Associated Press}}</ref> In February 2009, he was elected President of the [[Serbian Olympic Committee]] for a four-year term<ref name="blicokserb">{{cite web |url=http://www.blic.rs/sport.php?id=80659 |title=Divac na čelu OKS naredne četiri godine |language=sr |date=February 24, 2009 |publisher=Blic |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090227115052/http://www.blic.rs/sport.php?id=80659 |archive-date=February 27, 2009 }}</ref> and re-elected in November 2012.<ref name=oks>{{cite web|url=http://sport.blic.rs/Ostali-sportovi/223132/Divac-ponovo-izabran-za-predsednika-Olimpijskog-komiteta-Srbije |title=Vlade Divac ponovo izabran za predsednika Olimpijskog komiteta |language=sr |trans-title=Vlade Divac re-elected for the President of the Olympic Committee|publisher=Beta |date= November 7, 2012}}</ref> In 2013, Divac received an honor from the World Sports Humanitarian Hall of Fame.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.nba.com/kings/news/divac_hall_of_fame08.html |title=Kings News Full |publisher=National Basketball Association |access-date=January 24, 2013}}</ref>

