'''Vincent Lamar Carter Jr.''' (born January 26, 1977) is an American former professional [[basketball]] player who played for 22 seasons in the [[National Basketball Association]] (NBA). He primarily played the [[shooting guard]] and [[small forward]] positions, and was known for his high flying dunks, scoring ability, and athleticism. An eight-time [[NBA All-Star|All-Star]] and a two-time [[All-NBA Team]] selection, he played 22 seasons in the NBA, the second longest after [[LeBron James]] for the [[List of NBA seasons played leaders|most seasons played in league history]].<ref>{{Cite web|last=McGregor|first=Gilbert|url=https://www.sportingnews.com/us/nba/news/most-seasons-lebron-james-nba-history/3f8df894de47295cb93a240d|title=Most seasons in the NBA: How LeBron James will make history with 2024 Lakers debut|website=[[The Sporting News]]|date=October 22, 2024|access-date=October 23, 2024}}</ref> He is also the only player to have played in the NBA in four different decades (1990s, 2000s, 2010s, 2020s).<ref name=":0" /><ref>{{cite web|title=Richard Jefferson Says Vince Carter Was More Talented Than LeBron James: "To Me, He Is The Most Talented Player I Have Ever Seen In My Life."|url=https://www.msn.com/en-us/sports/nba/richard-jefferson-says-vince-carter-was-more-talented-than-lebron-james-to-me-he-is-the-most-talented-player-i-have-ever-seen-in-my-life%e2%80%9d/ar-AAOwMkt?li=BBnbfcL|website=MSN.com|language=en}}</ref> In 2024, Carter was inducted into the [[Naismith Memorial Basketball Hall of Fame]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.nba.com/news/hall-of-fame-class-of-2024 |title=Naismith Basketball Hall of Fame announces 13 inductees for Class of 2024 |date=April 6, 2024 |website=NBA.com |accessdate=April 6, 2024}}</ref>

Carter entertained crowds with his leaping ability and slam dunks, earning him nicknames such as "'''Vinsanity'''", "'''Air Canada'''" (a play on the [[Air Canada|Canadian airline of the same name]]), and "'''Half Man, Half Amazing'''".{{Efn|Referenced in lyric containing this phrase in "[[It Ain't Hard to Tell]]" and "[[Nas Is Like]]", songs by rapper [[Nas]].}} He has been ranked as the greatest [[Slam dunk|dunker]] of all time by numerous players, journalists, and by the NBA.<ref>Multiple sources:
*{{cite web|last1=Ojeda|first1=Louis|title=NBA's greatest dunker of all time|url=https://www.foxsports.com/southwest/gallery/nba-s-greatest-dunkers-of-all-time-021016|website=FOXSports.com}}
*{{cite web|last1=Bitanga|first1=Mike|date=February 11, 2014|title=Vince Carter: The Greatest Dunkers of All Time|url=https://www.hardwoodandhollywood.com/2014/02/11/vinsanity-greatest-dunker-time/|website=hardwoodandhollywood.com}}
